      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Elimination of key risk factors such as dyslipidemia and hypertension is important for reducing cardiovascular events later in life. A medical history, physical examination, and laboratory determination of lipid and glycosylated hemoglobin levels provide a good assessment of cardiovascular risk. A statin is first-line therapy for reducing LDL-C, which is the primary lipid target in most patients. High-dose statin therapy may be required to reach desired target levels. The choice of initial antihypertensive therapy is based on patient comorbidities and drug side effects; however, most patients require combination antihypertensive therapy to reach goal. The combination of this multifactorial risk approach along with smoking cessation and modification of other risk factors should complement current and future cardiovascular care for men."},
